<article class="env-lemma" id="0B67">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B67">Lemma <span data-tag="0B67">15.68.15</span>.</a> Let $A \to B$ be a ring map. Let $K^\bullet $ be a complex of $B$-modules. Let $a, b \in \mathbf{Z}$. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$K^\bullet $ has tor amplitude in $[a, b]$ as a complex of $A$-modules, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$K^\bullet _\mathfrak q$ has tor amplitude in $[a, b]$ as a complex of $A_\mathfrak p$-modules for every prime $\mathfrak q \subset B$ with $\mathfrak p = A \cap \mathfrak q$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$K^\bullet _\mathfrak m$ has tor amplitude in $[a, b]$ as a complex of $A_\mathfrak p$-modules for every maximal ideal $\mathfrak m \subset B$ with $\mathfrak p = A \cap \mathfrak m$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume (3) and let $M$ be an $A$-module. Then $H^ i = H^ i(K^\bullet \otimes _ A^\mathbf {L} M)$ is a $B$-module and $(H^ i)_\mathfrak m = H^ i(K^\bullet _\mathfrak m \otimes _{A_\mathfrak p}^\mathbf {L} M_\mathfrak p)$. Hence $H^ i = 0$ for $i \not\in [a, b]$ by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00HN" data-tag="00HN">10.23.1</a>. Thus (3) $\Rightarrow $ (1). We omit the proofs of (1) $\Rightarrow $ (2) and (2) $\Rightarrow $ (3). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>